**Hospitalist Progress Note**
Providence Saint Joseph's Medical Center
Benchmark Hospitalist Group

{{DEMOGRAPHICS TIGHT BLOCK (HARDWIRED): the eight lines from '**Patient Name:**' through '**Primary Care Physician:**' MUST be output as ONE tight block — each field on its own consecutive line with NO blank line, empty line, or spacer between ANY of them.}}
**Patient Name:** {{name}}
**Age:** {{age}}
**DOB:** {{dob}}
**MRN:** {{mrn}}
**Date of Admission:** {{admission_date}}
**Date of Service:** @TD@
**Attending:** {{attending_physician}}
**Primary Care Physician:** {{primary_care_physician}}

**CC:** {{brief chief complaint inferred from the data; only if the patient was transferred from another hospital, add the transferring hospital name and transfer date}}

**HOSPITAL COURSE**
{{SIMPLIFIED — 2 to 4 short sentences: reason for admission, key events, procedures, and named consultants, then today's interval update and trajectory. Plain language; NO units; at most one or two key values. Only information supported by provided data; no invented details; no source attribution.}}

**Overnight Events:** {{1 to 3 short sentences from today's provided data — clinical changes, trends in plain words, oxygen or hemodynamic changes, rapid responses, new consultant input, antibiotic changes; any NEW positive culture in bold. If no overnight information is provided, write exactly: No acute overnight events.}}
{{ONLY if PT/OT/SLP updates are provided today, add one short line here: 'Therapy Updates (PT/OT/SLP): [update]' — omit this line entirely when none are provided.}}

**ASSESSMENT & PLAN**

{{DIAGNOSTIC SPECIFICITY & CODING RULES — apply to every diagnosis TITLE (titles are NOT simplified):
Group related diagnoses together. Make every diagnosis as specific as possible with laterality when available. Do NOT write ICD codes anywhere in the note.
- Acute Encephalopathy: always specify etiology — 'due to [cause]'
- Malnutrition: detect when criteria met; specify mild / moderate / severe in title
- Sepsis: always specify organism and/or source in title
- Type 2 Myocardial Infarction: always spell out in full — never abbreviate as 'Type 2 MI'
- AKI: always specify with or without ATN/vasomotor nephropathy in title
- SIRS: list as a separate active problem; title specifies with or without organ dysfunction
- Antibiotics: in the dash lines name the antibiotic and, when start date and duration are provided, add the day count compactly (e.g., 'Continue ceftriaxone, day 3 of 7.') — no guideline names
- Catheter-associated infection: title specifies catheter-associated or not
- GI/any bleeding: title specifies if due to or associated with anticoagulant; name the anticoagulant only if it was in use prior to the bleeding}}

{{A&P FORMAT (HARDWIRED — SIMPLIFIED STYLE): Each problem is EXACTLY: one BOLD title line — the literal character '#' immediately followed by the diagnosis with NO space after '#', bolded with '**' only, exact form '**#Acute kidney injury due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**' — no heading marks; then, on the IMMEDIATELY following line with NO blank line in between, the dash lines, each on its own line starting with the literal three characters '\- ' (backslash, hyphen, space). The backslash is REQUIRED — it stops the chat interface from turning the line into a '•' bullet glyph. No blank lines between dash lines within a problem; exactly ONE blank line before the next title. TITLES ARE NOT SIMPLIFIED: keep etiology linkage, a factual trajectory word (improving/resolved/worsening), and [POA]; NO certainty words. DASH LINES ARE SIMPLE per SIMPLIFIED_AP_STYLE_LOCK: one short plain sentence each (about 12 words or fewer); typically 4 to 7 per active problem — 1 to 3 brief findings first, then brief actions ('Continue IV fluids.', 'Trend lactate every 6 hours.', 'Avoid nephrotoxic medications.'). NO units; prefer trend words; at most 1 to 2 key values per problem without units ('Hemoglobin 5 on admission, now 8.'). NO dates unless essential. Medications by generic NAME only (route only when it distinguishes, e.g., 'IV pantoprazole') — NO dose, frequency, or units in A&P lines (full sigs are in ACTIVE MEDS); state a dose only when the plan IS a dose change. Cross-reference related problems instead of repeating ('Related to lactic acidosis.', 'Trend lactate as above.'). NO 'Assessment:' label, paragraph, or 'Plan' subheader. NO hypothetical 'If X, then Y' lines. NO two-midnight or observation status statement — progress notes never carry one.}}

{{FORMAT & STYLE EXAMPLE — BEGIN. This block defines the REQUIRED structure, brevity, and spacing for EVERY problem block. It is a formatting example ONLY. NEVER copy its diagnoses, values, medications, or findings into the note; all real content comes exclusively from the provided input data.

**#Lactic acidosis due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**
\- Lactate improved from greater than 15 to 2.8 with resuscitation.
\- Hypotension and tachycardia after poor oral intake and vomiting.
\- Blood cultures pending. Urinalysis and chest radiograph without infectious source.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Continue empiric ceftriaxone.
\- Trend lactate every 6 hours.
\- Monitor hemodynamics and intake/output.

**#Anion gap metabolic acidosis, improving [POA]**
\- Related to lactic acidosis.
\- Bicarbonate improved after IV fluids.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Monitor metabolic panel.
\- Trend lactate as above.

**#Acute kidney injury due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**
\- Creatinine 1.44, improved from 1.83 after IV fluids.
\- Urinalysis without infection.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Monitor intake/output and metabolic panel.
\- Avoid nephrotoxic medications.

**#Anemia with history of gastrointestinal bleeding [POA]**
\- Hemoglobin 9.9 on admission, stable.
\- No active signs of gastrointestinal bleeding.
\- Continue IV pantoprazole.
\- Monitor CBC.
\- Goal hemoglobin greater than 7.

**#Alcohol use disorder [POA]**
\- Recent alcohol binge; last drink four days before admission.
\- CIWA score 0.
\- Continue CIWA protocol and lorazepam as needed.
\- Continue thiamine and folate.

**#Chronic Problems**

**#Gastroesophageal reflux disease**
\- Continue IV pantoprazole.
\- Resume home pantoprazole when tolerating oral intake.

FORMAT & STYLE EXAMPLE — END. Every real problem block below MUST match this structure, brevity, and spacing. This example governs ONLY the ASSESSMENT & PLAN section — every other section MUST still be rendered per FULL_NOTE_ALL_SECTIONS_REQUIRED_LOCK.}}

**#[Dx Title] [POA if the diagnosis was present on admission, otherwise leave empty]**
\- {{1 to 3 brief findings lines in plain words — what happened, the key result, the trend; at most 1 to 2 key values, no units.}}
\- {{brief action lines starting with a verb — generic medication names only (no doses), monitoring, workup, avoidance; typically 4 to 7 lines total per active problem.}}

{{Repeat the above diagnosis block for ALL active inpatient problems, exactly ONE blank line between problems. Stable chronic problems go ONLY in the Chronic Problems section below.}}

**#Chronic Problems**

**#[Chronic Dx Title]**
\- {{1 to 3 short lines: status in plain words; continued or held medications by name only; when to resume home medications.}}

{{Repeat the chronic problem block for ALL stable chronic problems, exactly ONE blank line between blocks.}}

**DVT Prophylaxis:** {{dvt_prophylaxis}}

**Code Status:** {{code_status}}

**Disposition:**
\- GMLOS: {{gmlos}}
\- Expected Day of Discharge (EDD): [{{admission_date}} + GMLOS]
\- Anticipated Discharge Date: [MM/DD/YYYY] {{infer daily using SW/CM/PT/OT disposition needs, placement/auth/DME timing, pending procedures and anticipated weekend/holiday delays, and clinical improvement/trajectory; if date changes since prior note, add ONE concise reason on this same line only}}
\- Barriers to Discharge: {{disposition_barriers}}{{cm_sw_discharge_planning_one_line_if_provided}}

**Vital Signs:** {{ONE line, values WITHOUT units except SpO2 percent and oxygen flow — e.g., 'Temp 98.6, BP 141/99, HR 101, RR 19, SpO2 91% on room air'; Tmax when provided; provided values only}}

**PHYSICAL EXAM**
{{PHYSICAL EXAM must never be a paragraph. Each system on its own line beginning with the literal three characters '\- ' (backslash, hyphen, space) exactly as formatted below. Short terse phrases; baseline for systems without provided findings; update only systems with provided abnormalities.}}
\- General: {{exam_general}}
\- HEENT: {{exam_heent}}
\- CV: {{exam_cv}}
\- Lungs: {{exam_lungs}}
\- Abd: {{exam_abd}}
\- Ext: {{exam_ext}}
\- Neuro: {{exam_neuro}}
\- Skin: {{exam_skin}}
\- Psych: {{exam_psych}}

**LABS & DIAGNOSTICS**
{{COMPACT, NO UNITS: today's labs grouped on short lines — e.g., 'CBC: WBC 9.98, Hgb 15.8, Plt 205' / 'BMP: Na 134, K 4.6, Cl 98, CO2 31, BUN 14, Cr 0.85, Glu 140' / 'Other: Lactate 2.8, Mg 2.5, Procalcitonin 0.07'. Values only — no units, no reference ranges, no (H)/(L) flags. If no labs are provided, write exactly: Labs: not provided}}
{{Microbiology: ONE line, ONLY if provided; new positive cultures in bold.}}
{{Imaging: ONE line per study, ONLY if provided — study name, date, and impression only (no technique, comparison, dose, or signature boilerplate).}}

**ACTIVE MEDS**
\- {{one medication per line: generic name, dose, route, frequency exactly as provided — dose units KEPT; standard abbreviations IV, PO, subQ, daily, BID, TID, QID, q6h, PRN; never QD, QOD, U, or IU; mark PRN medications with PRN and the indication when provided. Never a paragraph or comma-separated list.}}

50 minutes required to complete the MDM above.

